---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro'
import PhaseBand from '../components/PhaseBand.astro'
import Section from '../components/Section.astro'
import HeroArt from '../components/HeroArt.astro'
---
<Base
  title="LunaTrack — period and cycle tracking"
  description="LunaTrack shows no ads on symptom logging, diary, or insights. Your device leads, the app works offline, and health data never reaches advertisers."
  path="/"
>
  <div class="lg:grid lg:grid-cols-[minmax(0,1fr)_17rem] lg:gap-12">
    <div>
      <PhaseBand height="14px" draw labelled class="max-w-md" />

      <h1 class="display mt-9 text-[2.6rem] sm:text-6xl">
        Track your cycle.<br />Read the fine print.
      </h1>

      <p class="prose-body mt-6 max-w-prose text-lg">
        No ads on symptom logging, your diary, or your insights — ever. That's the
        one promise here that's easy to check for yourself.
      </p>

      <div class="mt-8 flex flex-wrap gap-3">
        <a
          href="/download"
          class="pressable inline-flex h-btn-h items-center rounded-btn bg-primary px-6 font-medium text-white hover:bg-menstrual"
        >Get it on Google Play</a>
        <a
          href="/features"
          class="pressable inline-flex h-btn-h items-center rounded-btn border border-outline px-6 font-medium hover:border-primary dark:border-white/20 dark:hover:border-white"
        >See everything it tracks</a>
      </div>
    </div>

    <aside class="mt-12 border-t border-outline pt-6 text-sm text-ink-muted lg:mt-2 lg:border-l lg:border-t-0 lg:pl-8 lg:pt-0 dark:border-white/10 dark:text-white/60">
      <HeroArt class="mx-auto mb-8 max-w-[15rem] lg:mb-7 lg:max-w-none" />
      <p class="prose-body">
        The band above is a 28-day cycle drawn to scale. Yours will differ —
        that variation is the whole reason a tracker keeps a history instead of
        a single number.
      </p>
      <p class="prose-body mt-4">
        Everything this site claims is written to be checkable. Where LunaTrack
        falls short, the <a href="/privacy" class="underline decoration-menstrual underline-offset-2">data page</a> says so.
      </p>
    </aside>
  </div>

  <Section title="Your device leads, the cloud follows">
    <p>
      Every log — periods, symptoms, mood, medication marks, weight, basal body
      temperature, ovulation test results — is written to a database on your
      device first. LunaTrack works fully offline, and that on-device copy is
      the source of truth; the cloud is a mirror of it, kept for the account
      LunaTrack requires so your logs follow you between devices. The device
      database is encrypted at rest with a key held in your OS keystore — the{' '}
      <a href="/privacy-policy" class="underline underline-offset-2">full privacy policy</a> spells out
      the one documented exception to that, along with everything else that
      syncs.
    </p>
  </Section>

  <Section title="What's inside">
    <p>
      Predictions, a daily log for symptoms and mood, medication marks, weight
      and BBT tracking, ovulation test results, reminders, an app lock, a
      doctor-ready PDF export, and a photo timeline. The{' '}
      <a href="/features" class="underline underline-offset-2">features page</a> covers all of it,
      including what each one does and doesn't do.
    </p>
  </Section>

  <Section title="Ads that stay out of the way">
    <p>
      The free version shows non-personalised ads through Google AdMob, and
      never on symptom logging, your diary, or your insights. Your health data
      is never sent to the ad network either way. If you'd rather not see ads
      at all, Premium is a one-time Google Play purchase that removes them
      everywhere — see <a href="/privacy" class="underline underline-offset-2">how your data is handled</a>{' '}
      for the details on both.
    </p>
  </Section>

  <Section title="Photo descriptions, off until you turn them on">
    <p>
      The photo timeline can describe what's in a picture you choose, using an
      outside image-recognition service. That's off by default, decided
      separately for each account and each device — read{' '}
      <a href="/privacy" class="underline underline-offset-2">what that involves</a> before you switch it on.
    </p>
  </Section>

  <Section title="Not sure about a date? Try a calculator first" phase="predicted">
    <p>
      Before you install anything, you can estimate your next period from a
      date you already know. The <a href="/tools" class="underline underline-offset-2">calculators
      page</a> runs the same cycle maths as the app, entirely in your browser —
      nothing you type into it is sent anywhere. Alongside the{' '}
      <a href="/tools/period-calculator" class="underline underline-offset-2">period
      calculator</a>, there's also an{' '}
      <a href="/tools/ovulation-calculator" class="underline underline-offset-2">ovulation
      calculator</a>, a{' '}
      <a href="/tools/cycle-length-calculator" class="underline underline-offset-2">cycle-length
      calculator</a>, and a{' '}
      <a href="/tools/due-date-calculator" class="underline underline-offset-2">due-date
      calculator</a>, all built the same way.
    </p>

    <figure class="mt-7 max-w-md">
      <div
        class="h-3 w-full rounded-full"
        style="background:linear-gradient(90deg,
          color-mix(in oklab, var(--color-predicted) 0%, transparent) 0%,
          var(--color-predicted) 28%,
          var(--color-menstrual) 50%,
          var(--color-predicted) 72%,
          color-mix(in oklab, var(--color-predicted) 0%, transparent) 100%)"
        role="img"
        aria-label="A predicted period drawn as a band that fades at both ends, showing that the estimate is a range rather than a single certain day."
      ></div>
      <figcaption class="mt-3 text-sm text-ink-muted dark:text-white/60">
        A prediction is a range, not a date. The calculators give you the
        middle; the faded ends are the part most apps don't draw.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>
  </Section>

  <Section title="Want the longer version of any of this?">
    <p>
      The <a href="/articles" class="underline underline-offset-2">articles library</a> covers the
      reasoning behind the calculators above, what counts as a normal cycle, and
      a checklist for reading any tracker's privacy policy — applied honestly to
      LunaTrack's own, including where it falls short. Every article carries
      dated sources at the end.
    </p>
  </Section>

  <section class="mt-20 border-t border-outline pt-10 dark:border-white/10">
    <h2 class="display-sm text-2xl">Try it</h2>
    <p class="prose-body mt-3 max-w-prose">
      <a href="/download" class="underline underline-offset-2">Download LunaTrack on Google Play</a>.
      It requires an account to sign in, and its{' '}
      <a href="/terms" class="underline underline-offset-2">terms of use</a> cover purchases and warranty.
    </p>
  </section>
</Base>
